  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nBrowsers are tuned for top-down HTML delivery, whereas many sites want shell-to-content delivery or modular HTML composition without requiring a server-side template runtime, build step, or client-side JS framework (see #2791):\n\n* Buildless DRY HTML: Reusing shared partials (headers, footers, navigation, icons) on static hosts or local setups.\n* Split caching: Serving a CDN-cached static shell while pulling in dynamic or personalized fragments (auth status, cart, regional pricing), or caching shared chrome across navigations while fetching only page-specific fragments.\n* Async subsections: Loading slower or secondary sections (comments, feeds, recommendations) independently without delaying the initial document response.\n* Cross-service composition: Combining HTML fragments across services directly into the document's layout and styling tree with built-in sanitization and scoped context boundaries instead of <iframe> isolation.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nToday authors either assemble everything on the server/edge (SSI, ESI, SSR frameworks) or ship JavaScript (fetch() + innerHTML/setHTML(), <h-include>, HTMX, Turbo Frames).\n\nPrevious attempts at declarative client-side includes (#2791) stalled on three problems that modern HTML primitives now address together:\n\n* Parser-blocking waterfalls: Out-of-order streaming via <template for=\"...\"> ([WICG/declarative-partial-updates](https://github.com/WICG/declarative-partial-updates/blob/main/patching-explainer.md)\n) decouples where a fetch is initiated from where its markup is inserted, allowing fragments to stream asynchronously into <?start name=\"...\">...<?end> or <?marker name=\"...\"> placeholders.\n\n* FOUC and layout shifts: <link rel=\"expect\" href=\"#...\" blocking=\"render\">, container space reservation, and proposed [hidden=\"while-loading\"](https://github.com/WICG/declarative-partial-updates/blob/main/partial-render-blocking-explainer.md)\n prevent half-styled flashes and layout shifts.\n\n* XSS: The Sanitizer API (removeUnsafe()) and CSP now provide a foundation for safe-by-default markup inclusion.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nSuggesting to add a `src` attribute that works together with `<template for>`.\n\nIt fetches the content from the `src` URL and streams it into the `for` location (alongside `nonce`, `referrerpolicy` etc).\nContent is sanitized by default (current sanitizer with `removeUnsafe`), but that behavior can be opted out with a `sanitize=unsafe` attribute.\n\n```html\n<header><?marker name=\"header\"></header>\n<main>\n  <section><?start name=\"feed\"><p>Loading feed...</p><?end></section>\n  <section><?start name=\"chart\"><p>Loading chart...</p><?end></section>\n</main>\n\n<!-- Sanitized by default (enclosing Sanitizer or empty Sanitizer + removeUnsafe()) -->\n<template for=\"header\" src=\"/partials/header.fragment\"></template>\n<template for=\"feed\" src=\"/partials/feed.fragment\"></template>\n\n<!-- Inherits ambient parser context as-is (allows scripts in main document, subject to CSP) -->\n<template for=\"chart\" src=\"/widgets/chart.fragment\" sanitize=\"unsafe\"></template>\n```\n\nee [explainer](https://github.com/WICG/declarative-partial-updates/blob/main/fragment-include-explainer.md) for more details. \n\n### Anything else?\n\n\n  - When both for and src are present on <template> (and shadowrootmode is not), the UA asynchronously fetches src and streams the parsed fragment into the target range (<?start name=\"...\">...<?end>) or marker (<?marker name=\"...\">) in the target parent's fragment-parsing context.\nWithout for, <template src=\"...\"> remains an ordinary inert <template>. Inline child content inside <template for src> is ignored (fallback content belongs between <?start> and <?end>).\n\n  - Nested <template for> and <template for src> work inside the fetched fragment, bounded strictly within that fragment's context so they cannot target markers in the outer document (with a nesting depth limit to prevent cycles).\nFetching (text/fragment, CORS, \"fragment\" destination):\nSupporting attributes on <template>: src, crossorigin, referrerpolicy, integrity, fetchpriority, and global nonce.\n\n  - Requests use Fetch mode \"cors\", same-origin credentials by default (crossorigin=\"use-credentials\" for cross-origin credentials), and request destination \"fragment\" (Sec-Fetch-Dest: fragment).\nResponses must have Content-Type: text/fragment (strict MIME check, no sniffing; text/html is rejected so full HTML error pages are never included and fragment endpoints do not render as active documents on direct navigation).\n\n  - Relative URLs inside the fragment resolve against the host document's document.baseURI (matching innerHTML, setHTML(), and inline <template for>).\n \n  - On pre-stream failure (network/HTTP error, wrong MIME type, CORS/CSP/SRI failure), the include aborts and any placeholder between <?start> and <?end> stays intact; both pre-stream and mid-stream failures emit an \"abort\" mutation record to opted-in observers (#13008).\n\n- CSP: A new fragment-src directive governs \"fragment\" requests, falling back to script-src (then default-src).\n\n### Open questions\n\n- Should pending <template for src> includes delay DOMContentLoaded (like <script defer>) or only window.onload (or depend on loading=\"lazy\")?\n- Should default safe mode also strip <meta http-equiv> (e.g. <meta http-equiv=\"refresh\">), either in <template for src> or by categorizing it as unsafe in the Sanitizer spec?\n- Should an empty for attribute (<template for src=\"...\">) be supported later as shorthand for in-place inclusion?",
